WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.834 --> 00:00:03.303
Here's a formula for you to remember.

00:00:03.303 --> 00:00:05.972
S equals P plus D plus R.

00:00:06.039 --> 00:00:09.242
What does that mean? Security is about prevention,

00:00:09.242 --> 00:00:11.111
detection and response.

00:00:11.111 --> 00:00:14.714
Remember the CIA triad I mentioned in the second video of this series?

00:00:14.948 --> 00:00:17.851
It's about confidentiality, integrity and availability.

00:00:17.851 --> 00:00:22.389
And I said everything we do in security is about trying to achieve one or more of those things.

00:00:22.889 --> 00:00:24.624
Well, that's kind of the

00:00:24.624 --> 00:00:27.160
“what” of cybersecurity.

00:00:27.427 --> 00:00:29.329
This is what we're trying to do.

00:00:29.329 --> 00:00:31.931
This equation is the “how”.

00:00:31.931 --> 00:00:34.200
This is how we're going to go about doing that.

00:00:34.467 --> 00:00:38.338
And that is with prevention, detection and response.

00:00:38.905 --> 00:00:42.275
Now, what we've covered as we've gone
through the domains up to this point:

00:00:42.542 --> 00:00:44.310
Identity and access management.

00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:48.915
Endpoint security, network security, application
security and data security.

00:00:49.182 --> 00:00:52.819
This is all largely been about prevention. Not 100%,

00:00:52.819 --> 00:00:56.723
but mostly with the controls that we put in place down here are about trying

00:00:56.723 --> 00:01:01.928
to prevent a data attack, a breach, any of those kinds of things.

00:01:02.162 --> 00:01:03.830
So that's what that's about.

00:01:03.830 --> 00:01:06.599
Now we're going to start looking at the other parts of the equation.

00:01:06.933 --> 00:01:11.671
Today in particular, we're going to focus on the D part of this--
this detection aspect.

00:01:12.005 --> 00:01:14.774
And then in the next video, we'll cover response.

00:01:15.075 --> 00:01:16.910
Now, how do we do detection?

00:01:16.910 --> 00:01:21.414
Well, it basically means
I need to get information from all of these different domains

00:01:21.414 --> 00:01:26.686
that I've been discussing
previously and feed them into some sort of monitoring engine.

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:29.956
So that's what we're going to be taking a look at. Monitoring.

00:01:30.156 --> 00:01:31.691
Then we're going to analyze.

00:01:31.691 --> 00:01:33.226
Then we're going to report.

00:01:33.226 --> 00:01:35.395
And then we're going to do this thing called threat hunting.

00:01:35.695 --> 00:01:38.264
This is what the purview of this area is.

00:01:38.598 --> 00:01:41.801
And then in that final video, we'll take a look at the response.

00:01:41.835 --> 00:01:45.972
What do I do with all of this information
once I realize that I've got a problem?

00:01:46.473 --> 00:01:49.442
Now, these two functions are largely done

00:01:49.442 --> 00:01:53.179
by an organization called the SOC, the Security Operations Center.

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:55.381
So bear in mind, this is kind of all

00:01:56.983 --> 00:02:00.320
coming together in that one organization
that's going to do that particular work.

00:02:00.854 --> 00:02:05.825
And what are the technologies that they're used to do,
this kind of detection, to do this kind of work?

00:02:06.092 --> 00:02:09.095
Well, it's basically two predominant things.

00:02:09.395 --> 00:02:12.765
It's a security information and event management system

00:02:13.233 --> 00:02:17.637
or an XDR, an extended detection and response system.

00:02:17.937 --> 00:02:20.940
We're going to take a look at both of those as we go through this video.

00:02:22.342 --> 00:02:25.645
Okay. Now, we've introduced this idea of detection.

00:02:25.845 --> 00:02:27.780
Let's go into it a little bit deeper.

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:30.650
And specifically, we're going to start off talking about this thing.

00:02:30.850 --> 00:02:34.587
The security information and event management system or SIEM.

00:02:34.621 --> 00:02:37.157
Some people pronounce it “seam”, you can choose to pronounce it

00:02:37.157 --> 00:02:39.792
however you like. So I'll call it a SIEM.

00:02:39.792 --> 00:02:42.061
What is a SIEM do? What's its purpose?

00:02:42.328 --> 00:02:47.267
Well, it's if you think of this way, we look at all
the different domains that we've talked about in the past.

00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:50.670
Each one of these could be a source of security information.

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:56.743
And in fact, what typically happens
this is not best practice-- it’s typical practice --is I have a console

00:02:56.943 --> 00:03:01.381
some sort of security management system
that is unique to that particular domain.

00:03:01.548 --> 00:03:06.553
The identity management security console, the access management

00:03:06.553 --> 00:03:11.157
security console, the endpoint management console
and so on and so forth.

00:03:11.491 --> 00:03:15.828
In fact, I've got multiples of those consoles
and really multiples of the individuals,

00:03:15.995 --> 00:03:18.898
the security operators, analysts that have to deal

00:03:18.898 --> 00:03:22.869
with those particular domains and have that specific domain knowledge.

00:03:23.336 --> 00:03:25.538
You can see this is very expensive.

00:03:25.538 --> 00:03:28.208
And also, what else is missing here?

00:03:28.474 --> 00:03:31.878
I don't have any consistent single view of what's happening.

00:03:32.178 --> 00:03:34.747
So the left hand doesn't know what the right hand is doing.

00:03:34.747 --> 00:03:37.650
And if an attacker comes in and hits one of these systems,

00:03:38.184 --> 00:03:40.787
it may generate alarms in lots of these systems.

00:03:40.787 --> 00:03:43.790
And then we've got a lot of people chasing all the same problem.

00:03:44.023 --> 00:03:45.558
It's not very efficient.

00:03:45.558 --> 00:03:48.261
So this is why the SIEM came into existence.

00:03:48.628 --> 00:03:53.566
Its purpose then was to say, instead of operating all of these things independently,

00:03:53.967 --> 00:03:56.869
let's come along with a layer on top of them

00:03:57.203 --> 00:04:00.673
where we take all of these information systems,

00:04:00.940 --> 00:04:03.743
feed it into a higher level system.

00:04:04.010 --> 00:04:05.044
That's our SIEM.

00:04:05.044 --> 00:04:09.682
So we create a database up here that is about this collection stuff.

00:04:10.049 --> 00:04:13.586
We're going to collect logs, we're going to collect alarms and events

00:04:13.586 --> 00:04:17.490
that occur, and we're going to collect flow data
that goes across the network.

00:04:17.790 --> 00:04:21.961
So each one of these systems would be able
to give us different types of information.

00:04:22.228 --> 00:04:25.598
We take all of that and we bring that up to the SIEM--

00:04:25.832 --> 00:04:29.402
big database, and then we start applying some analytics to it.

00:04:29.869 --> 00:04:34.674
One of the things we're going to do is correlate
because as I mentioned, a single attack

00:04:34.707 --> 00:04:40.346
might generate an alarm in multiples of these domains
and across multiples of these systems.

00:04:40.613 --> 00:04:45.752
So what I'd like to be able to do
is not see this as four different events or four different alarms.

00:04:46.085 --> 00:04:47.820
I want to see this as a single.

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:51.758
So one of the first things the SIEM will do after it's
collected is correlate

00:04:51.824 --> 00:04:55.862
all that information
and get it down to a smaller, more manageable subset.

00:04:56.496 --> 00:05:00.333
Another thing we're going to do
then is start analyzing the information we have.

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:04.771
We're going to take a look at, for instance, rules
that are based upon our security policies.

00:05:05.071 --> 00:05:10.343
I might say if a certain condition,
such as traffic coming from a particular geo

00:05:10.777 --> 00:05:13.846
and then it meets some other criteria,

00:05:13.846 --> 00:05:16.949
like someone tries to log in too many times or something like that

00:05:17.250 --> 00:05:21.888
and some other criteria.
So we can start building these very complex rules.

00:05:22.088 --> 00:05:25.725
And a good SIEM will have a lot of these
that already come out of the box.

00:05:25.958 --> 00:05:28.728
But I can also customize them and build all these rules.

00:05:28.995 --> 00:05:32.365
Then if all of those things happen, I want to take a specific action.

00:05:32.532 --> 00:05:34.100
I want to generate an alarm.

00:05:34.100 --> 00:05:36.502
I want it to be of a certain priority.

00:05:36.736 --> 00:05:39.572
I want it to be assigned to a particular person.

00:05:39.872 --> 00:05:42.775
And so ultimately, what I'm going to do with those priorities

00:05:42.942 --> 00:05:46.713
is I'm going to assign them as well as high, medium and low.

00:05:47.013 --> 00:05:49.048
The SIEM system ought to be able to do that.

00:05:49.048 --> 00:05:52.085
And I could do that from a rule or I could have the system do that

00:05:52.085 --> 00:05:54.921
automatically based upon its confidence level,

00:05:55.121 --> 00:05:58.458
based upon calculations that it's done and things of that sort.

00:05:59.025 --> 00:06:02.562
Another thing in the analysis I want to do is look for anomalies.

00:06:02.962 --> 00:06:06.999
So these are things where I know I'm looking for a specific use

00:06:06.999 --> 00:06:12.071
case, a specific set of examples,
a specific set of indicators of compromise.

00:06:12.472 --> 00:06:15.875
And when I see those, then I know I've got a problem, or at least I know

00:06:15.875 --> 00:06:19.312
I have a high probability
there's a problem and someone should investigate.

00:06:20.146 --> 00:06:24.684
In this case, I may be looking to say,
just tell me if something looks weird.

00:06:25.051 --> 00:06:27.453
I don't know what weird is. You figure it out.

00:06:27.787 --> 00:06:31.691
And so this is where things like artificial intelligence,
in particular machine

00:06:31.691 --> 00:06:37.697
learning is particularly good
because it can find patterns that we might not otherwise find. Feed it

00:06:37.730 --> 00:06:42.268
tons of this kind of information,
and then tell it to look for what's the anomaly.

00:06:42.602 --> 00:06:48.207
And a particular technology we do we use to do
this is called user behavior analytics.

00:06:48.541 --> 00:06:52.044
So UBA might leverage these underlying technologies

00:06:52.278 --> 00:06:54.981
as a way to find what's the thing that doesn't belong.

00:06:55.314 --> 00:06:58.518
Why is this user doing something different than all of his peers?

00:06:58.885 --> 00:07:04.023
Or why is certain things happening at certain times
when we don't expect them to happen?

00:07:04.724 --> 00:07:07.293
So that's looking for the anomalies.

00:07:07.293 --> 00:07:09.695
Here we say, this is what I'm looking for; here

00:07:09.695 --> 00:07:12.932
I say, tell me for something
that I don't really know what I'm looking for.

00:07:13.366 --> 00:07:16.669
And then ultimately I look for trends. I want to see

00:07:16.669 --> 00:07:22.542
because I want to generate reports to management to say,
because remember, this whole organization

00:07:22.809 --> 00:07:27.313
I mentioned in the previous portion is the SOC,
the security operations center.

00:07:27.313 --> 00:07:31.717
And the SOC wants to know,
are we doing better this month than we were last month?

00:07:32.118 --> 00:07:34.620
Are we detecting more alarms? Are we not?

00:07:34.921 --> 00:07:37.023
Are we resolving those more quickly?

00:07:37.023 --> 00:07:43.996
And so the reporting of all of this, it would be important to know,
These are some of the major functions then of a SIEM.

00:07:43.996 --> 00:07:47.500
It's about trying to reduce the footprint that we have down here

00:07:47.767 --> 00:07:52.972
and give us a single point
where I can look and see the visibility of all of my systems,

00:07:53.272 --> 00:07:58.044
gather all of that information,
bring it up and do these kinds of analysis activities.

00:07:59.645 --> 00:08:01.714
Okay, we've just covered the SIEM.

00:08:01.948 --> 00:08:05.718
Now let's take a look at the other technology I mentioned, XDR.

00:08:06.052 --> 00:08:08.221
This is extended detection and response.

00:08:08.588 --> 00:08:13.893
Let's do a little compare and contrast
of these two different technologies so we can see how they fit together.

00:08:14.193 --> 00:08:17.063
Is it really SIEM versus XDR or not?

00:08:17.697 --> 00:08:18.698
We'll see.

00:08:18.698 --> 00:08:22.068
So, first of all, SIEMs, these again came into existence--

00:08:22.201 --> 00:08:26.239
Largely, the vendors that did these came from one of two different camps.

00:08:26.606 --> 00:08:29.709
They were either log management vendors and they would the idea

00:08:29.709 --> 00:08:32.278
was they take the system logs from all the different

00:08:32.745 --> 00:08:36.048
devices, operating systems, databases, applications,

00:08:36.349 --> 00:08:41.320
and manage all of those things and bring them up to some centralized database.

00:08:41.521 --> 00:08:43.956
And then we do the analysis I mentioned previously.

00:08:44.390 --> 00:08:47.527
Or they were focused on the network side of things.

00:08:47.727 --> 00:08:51.931
So it's network behavior, anomaly detection, this kind of technology.

00:08:52.231 --> 00:08:58.137
Most of the SIEM vendors came either from the log management
or the network management view of security,

00:08:58.437 --> 00:09:02.542
and the SIEM was designed to basically be able to reach across
both of those.

00:09:03.142 --> 00:09:07.446
Well, the SIEMs could always do more than that,
but that was where they traditionally came from.

00:09:07.780 --> 00:09:11.851
How about this newer technology called XDR, extended detection response?

00:09:12.084 --> 00:09:16.389
It grew out of a thing called endpoint detection and response.

00:09:16.756 --> 00:09:19.892
So we already talked about how we did detection
and response here in the SIEM.

00:09:20.293 --> 00:09:23.863
The idea here was we're taking most of the information up.

00:09:23.863 --> 00:09:27.366
It was kind of a bottoms up approach. With the XDR,

00:09:27.600 --> 00:09:30.269
it's really more of a top down. And here's what I mean.

00:09:30.836 --> 00:09:34.106
What we would do with an EDR system is we would actually install

00:09:34.106 --> 00:09:39.111
some kind of capability,
some kind of agent on each one of these systems,

00:09:39.111 --> 00:09:44.050
and that would sit there and would do detection
and would do a certain level of response.

00:09:44.283 --> 00:09:48.454
And the idea here is we're pushing the the actions down.

00:09:48.854 --> 00:09:51.624
It's more of a instead of a “let's bring everything up

00:09:51.624 --> 00:09:55.895
and then take action”, let's do as much as we can
and automate the response

00:09:56.262 --> 00:09:59.265
there on the platform as close to the source of the outage,

00:09:59.265 --> 00:10:02.702
as close to the source of the attack as we can make it.

00:10:03.069 --> 00:10:05.838
And that's what this did. With XDR, though

00:10:05.838 --> 00:10:08.941
we still need an ability to bring this information up.

00:10:09.241 --> 00:10:13.412
And this would be from servers, from desktops, from laptops.

00:10:13.679 --> 00:10:16.682
Those are the systems that we're trying to enforce policy on

00:10:16.916 --> 00:10:20.119
and look for anomalous behavior and things of that sort.

00:10:20.486 --> 00:10:24.957
So the EDR capabilities basically needed a way to report up

00:10:25.257 --> 00:10:27.960
and so that they could all give a more comprehensive view.

00:10:28.127 --> 00:10:32.498
And that's really where XDR came into
existence, was to do those kinds of things.

00:10:33.032 --> 00:10:38.371
Now, it turns out you could take an XDR system
and read all of these endpoint devices into it.

00:10:38.738 --> 00:10:44.043
You could actually even take the information from the SIEM
and forward it into an XDR,

00:10:44.276 --> 00:10:48.347
just as you could have taken the endpoint information
and fed it to the SIEM.

00:10:48.547 --> 00:10:51.784
So there's a lot of different ways that you can make these things work.

00:10:52.184 --> 00:10:55.454
But what's really interesting is that some vendors have come up with

00:10:55.454 --> 00:11:00.393
this idea is we'll keep both of these here,
but we're going to add a capability

00:11:00.393 --> 00:11:04.130
here to the XDR that's called Federated Search.

00:11:04.530 --> 00:11:07.733
And what Federated Search does is it says,

00:11:07.733 --> 00:11:12.304
I want to look for particular indicators of compromise or particular

00:11:12.304 --> 00:11:16.442
incidents, particular alerts, particular conditions.

00:11:16.742 --> 00:11:20.946
And I'm going to take those
and I'm going to say I'm going to query all my systems and say,

00:11:21.213 --> 00:11:25.051
do any of you have these kind of conditions
happening on your system right now?

00:11:25.685 --> 00:11:28.621
The advantage to that is I don't need all of the data

00:11:29.388 --> 00:11:33.325
pre-fetched and stored in advance in some big database.

00:11:33.693 --> 00:11:35.928
I go out and get it just in time.

00:11:35.928 --> 00:11:40.199
So we leave the data in place
and then we go out and gather it just as we need it.

00:11:40.299 --> 00:11:43.202
And a federated search basically tells each one of these systems,

00:11:43.369 --> 00:11:47.406
search your local database of information and see if there's a problem

00:11:47.573 --> 00:11:51.243
that matches the specific conditions that I'm spelling out.

00:11:51.510 --> 00:11:53.913
And if you have that, then report this back up.

00:11:54.280 --> 00:11:56.015
And that's the way these things work.

00:11:56.015 --> 00:11:58.918
It's a lot like the card game that a lot of kids play

00:11:58.918 --> 00:12:01.721
called Go Fish, where you say, does anybody have any threes?

00:12:01.954 --> 00:12:05.291
And everyone looks in their hand to see if they have any three cards.

00:12:05.291 --> 00:12:07.593
And if they do, then they have to turn that in.

00:12:07.860 --> 00:12:09.095
It's the same thing here.

00:12:09.095 --> 00:12:14.400
We're saying everyone run a search on your system locally
and then only report the results.

00:12:14.700 --> 00:12:18.571
It's much more efficient, but in fact, we kind of need both of these

00:12:18.971 --> 00:12:23.609
because the SIEM is particularly good at doing alarms
since all the information is coming up.

00:12:23.943 --> 00:12:26.245
But what we want to do is have high quality alarms.

00:12:26.278 --> 00:12:28.714
We don't want to just have tons and tons of information there.

00:12:29.181 --> 00:12:32.451
SIEMs tend to get more expensive the more information you feed into them.

00:12:33.085 --> 00:12:37.757
XDRs get around that problem by saying leave most of the data here
and I'll go fetch it just in time.

00:12:38.157 --> 00:12:40.826
But still, the XDR operator

00:12:40.826 --> 00:12:44.063
needs to know that there's a reason they need to go out
and look in the first place.

00:12:44.330 --> 00:12:48.134
So an alarm coming in from a SIEM might be a trigger to

00:12:48.400 --> 00:12:50.903
then cause an investigation to occur.

00:12:51.270 --> 00:12:54.039
So it's really not XDR versus SIEM.

00:12:54.273 --> 00:12:57.409
I want to leave you with the point that it's XDR plus.

00:12:57.777 --> 00:13:01.580
SIEM. These two work together and can complement each other

00:13:01.580 --> 00:13:04.650
and be part of a stronger security response.

00:13:05.851 --> 00:13:06.786
Okay.

00:13:06.852 --> 00:13:09.688
Now, we've talked about the SIEM and XDR

00:13:09.688 --> 00:13:13.893
technologies, which basically allow us to monitor, to analyze

00:13:13.893 --> 00:13:17.730
and report on the stuff that we see happening in our environment.

00:13:18.564 --> 00:13:20.232
Now let's talk about hunting.

00:13:20.232 --> 00:13:21.634
What is hunting about?

00:13:21.634 --> 00:13:24.904
Well, the reason we want to do this in the first place is

00:13:25.137 --> 00:13:27.506
this is an attack scenario, a timeline.

00:13:28.007 --> 00:13:30.976
And the first thing the bad guy does is reconnaissance.

00:13:31.310 --> 00:13:34.013
They basically check out your site, they case the joint.

00:13:34.013 --> 00:13:36.182
They try to figure out where your weak points are.

00:13:36.182 --> 00:13:39.251
So they're going to spend some time doing that initially.

00:13:39.718 --> 00:13:44.323
Then, according to Ponemon Institute’s Cost of a Data Breach survey,

00:13:44.857 --> 00:13:49.995
there's a delay in time until we have the mean-time-to-identify (MTTI).

00:13:50.162 --> 00:13:55.835
In other words, the guy attacks me at this point
after he's finished his reconnaissance, he goes in.

00:13:56.268 --> 00:14:01.874
Now, how long does it take before the organization is aware
that they've been attacked?

00:14:02.241 --> 00:14:06.245
Well, it turns out this is on the order of 200 days.

00:14:06.612 --> 00:14:10.082
That's a huge problem, because imagine if a bad guy

00:14:10.082 --> 00:14:14.320
was in your house for 200 days
before you realized that you had been broken into.

00:14:15.154 --> 00:14:20.392
And then the mean-time-to-contain (MTTC), that is, after
I am aware that there's a problem,

00:14:20.426 --> 00:14:22.561
how long before I actually have it fixed?

00:14:23.028 --> 00:14:26.165
Now we're taking a look at it about another 70 days.

00:14:26.165 --> 00:14:28.367
You put those two together, 270 days.

00:14:28.567 --> 00:14:32.071
It's the better part of a year that the since when you were attacked

00:14:32.271 --> 00:14:34.740
until you finally have recovered from all of this.

00:14:35.140 --> 00:14:37.142
So what would we like to be able to do in this?

00:14:37.443 --> 00:14:41.213
I'd like to be able to move awareness back earlier into this.

00:14:41.247 --> 00:14:44.984
If I can't completely prevent
the attack, at least become aware of it sooner.

00:14:45.251 --> 00:14:48.787
And the way we do that is with threat hunting.

00:14:49.421 --> 00:14:53.792
Now, threat hunting, as compared to a basic investigation.

00:14:53.959 --> 00:14:56.595
With an investigation, we're reacting.

00:14:58.430 --> 00:15:01.033
So the system is giving me an alarm,

00:15:01.500 --> 00:15:04.036
a guy has broken in and now I'm doing

00:15:04.036 --> 00:15:07.239
the forensic investigation to find out what happened.

00:15:07.640 --> 00:15:10.542
That's what we typically do with SIEM and XDR tools.

00:15:10.943 --> 00:15:14.847
But there's something else we could do,
and that's this idea of threat hunting,

00:15:15.047 --> 00:15:17.216
where I'm going to be more proactive.

00:15:18.751 --> 00:15:23.689
I'm going to basically use the skills, the experience

00:15:24.089 --> 00:15:26.959
and the instincts of a skilled

00:15:27.092 --> 00:15:30.596
cybersecurity analyst who has seen everything--

00:15:30.729 --> 00:15:35.834
hopefully --and kind of comes up with what is essentially a hypothesis.

00:15:36.201 --> 00:15:39.505
They say, I wonder if someone has done this or that or the other thing.

00:15:39.905 --> 00:15:41.507
We don't have an alarm yet.

00:15:41.507 --> 00:15:43.876
No one has told us that we've been broken into.

00:15:44.143 --> 00:15:50.149
But I want to get ahead of this
before anyone even allows the alarm to be sounded.

00:15:50.582 --> 00:15:53.786
So they develop a hypothesis based upon their experience

00:15:53.786 --> 00:15:57.256
and their instincts about what would someone go after?

00:15:57.489 --> 00:15:59.191
How might they attack us?

00:15:59.191 --> 00:16:01.060
What kinds of things would they do?

00:16:01.060 --> 00:16:05.030
We're looking at the way other attackers are breaching

00:16:05.030 --> 00:16:09.368
networks and systems and using that in our hypothesis as well.

00:16:09.868 --> 00:16:13.906
And the threat hunter then uses tools like these,

00:16:14.340 --> 00:16:16.742
the SIEM and the XDR, to go off

00:16:16.976 --> 00:16:20.779
and look for searches and look for indicators of compromise.

00:16:21.213 --> 00:16:25.217
And if they do it correctly, what we end up with is early detection.

00:16:25.584 --> 00:16:29.421
We basically move the bar back. In a perfect world,

00:16:29.588 --> 00:16:34.259
we'd be able to detect future crimes
and we'd arrest the bad guys before they even break in.

00:16:34.626 --> 00:16:36.195
But we don't live in that world.

00:16:36.195 --> 00:16:39.331
The next best thing we can do is try to find out as close

00:16:39.331 --> 00:16:42.634
to the attack as possible if we can't prevent it at all.

00:16:43.435 --> 00:16:46.472
Now, what we've done with this so far is we've talked

00:16:46.472 --> 00:16:49.575
about the detection aspect of all of these things.

00:16:50.175 --> 00:16:55.381
What we want to do in the final video
in the series is talk about response.

00:16:55.681 --> 00:16:58.550
So make sure you don't miss it.

00:16:59.551 --> 00:17:00.586
Thanks for watching.

00:17:00.586 --> 00:17:04.456
Please remember to hit like and subscribe
and don't miss the notify bell

00:17:04.523 --> 00:17:08.127
so that you don't miss any videos in this series.

